<h2>What a dental implant actually is</h2>
<p>A dental implant — sometimes called a <strong>tooth implant</strong> — is a small titanium post that takes the place of a tooth root. Once it integrates with your jawbone, it supports a custom-made crown that looks, feels, and functions like a natural tooth. Implants don't decay, they don't require grinding down nearby teeth like a bridge does, and they help preserve the bone in your jaw.</p>

<h2>What to expect</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li><strong>Consultation &amp; 3D scan.</strong> We use a CBCT scan to map your jaw and plan the implant position precisely.</li>
  <li><strong>Implant placement.</strong> The titanium post is placed under local anesthesia (sedation available). Most single-implant cases take under an hour.</li>
  <li><strong>Healing &amp; integration.</strong> Over the next 3–6 months, your bone fuses to the implant. You go about your normal life during this time.</li>
  <li><strong>Final crown.</strong> A custom crown is attached to the implant. You leave with a tooth that looks like it grew there.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When implants make sense</h2>
<p>Implants are an excellent option if you've lost a tooth to injury, decay, or gum disease and want a permanent replacement. They're also the standard of care when an existing bridge or partial is failing and you'd like a more stable solution.</p>

<h2>Dental implant vs. bridge vs. removable partial</h2>
<p>For a single missing tooth (or a few), there are three common ways to fill the gap. Here's how they compare — Dr. Phillips will help you choose what fits your mouth and budget.</p>
<div class="roadmap-table-wrap">
<table class="roadmap-table">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th scope="col">Feature</th>
      <th scope="col">Dental implant</th>
      <th scope="col">Fixed bridge</th>
      <th scope="col">Removable partial</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">Replaces the tooth root</th><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Affects neighboring teeth</th><td>No — stands on its own</td><td>Yes — they're ground down to hold it</td><td>Clasps rest on them</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Protects jawbone</th><td>Yes — preserves bone</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Feels like</th><td>A natural tooth</td><td>Fixed, but not rooted</td><td>Removable, can move</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Typical lifespan</th><td>Decades — often a lifetime</td><td>~10–15 years</td><td>~5–8 years</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Relative cost</th><td>Higher up front, lower over time</td><td>Middle</td><td>Lowest up front</td></tr>
